From: Erfan Abdi <erfangplus@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Kemu's code-cost tier selection (kuloPo a2a2de1a): promote loops, score the rest on emitted x86 code

When a hot guest region is translated, the translator decided between the lite and the heavy optimizer tier by the region's guest instruction count. The Kemu fork's
commit a2a2de1a replaces that rule (the test files and their Android.bp entries left out; the assembler moved under cpu_emulation/ in this tree, and `Call` is
`CallRel` here, so two hunks were redone by hand):
- the assembler records, for every emitted instruction, a relative cost weight and its control flow (gen_asm.py generates it for the generated instructions,
  the hand-written ones in x86_32_or_x86_64.h/x86_64.h call TrackInsnCost): MachineCode(collect_cost) keeps them (code_cost.h/.cc, AnalyzeCodeCost).
- translator_x86_64.cc: a fully translated region with a captured loop is promoted to heavy whatever its length; other hot regions translate a lite candidate as
  well and take the heavy code only when its score is below 90% of the lite one's (same guest range, same number of back edges); a region the cost cannot
  rate keeps the length rule. `BERBERIS_GEARUP_POLICY=legacy` (or the property berberis.gearup_policy) restores the old rule; unset or "cost" is the new
  default. Counters (g_gearup_cost_stats) and TRACE lines show the decisions.
This changes which regions run in the heavy tier, where PGR's home-scene noise comes from (berberis.mode.<package> keeps an app out of it, 0008): check PGR's
home scene before shipping. Applies after 0024. Module libberberis_arm64.

